The files:

  passwd
  group

can be used to support archaic Crypt passwords for data sources.
Breaking such short passwords is trival. Setting a password on
a dataset via these files merely servers to remind people that
your data source isn't public and that they shouldn't attempt to
publish it's content. A determined person will find a way around
such a simple scheme. This method is supported for convienence
and is reasonably safe as most science data are eventually released
to the public anyway.  

*Do no use this method for any site that allows for data uploads*

In addition to being easy to break, users can't change thier password
unless they have shell access to the server, thus passwords saved in
`passwd` tend to live forever.  

The preferred alternative is to set the entire das sever behind an
OAuth2.0 wall.  

Now that you've been warned, if you wish to use the passwd and group
files anyway here's how it's done:

passwd
------
The password strings are maintained by the htpasswd command:

1. To set your password, or create a das2 Account if you don't have one:

  htpasswd -d %(ETC_DIR)s/passwd USERNAME
  
  Currently the das2-pyserver only understands the crypt hash, thus the
  '-d' option is needed.
  
2. To remove an account:

  htpasswd -D %(ETC_DIR)s/passwd USERNAME
  
In the future the code for htpasswd will be modified to create a 
das2passwd program, that accounts for user email addresses.

group
-----
Group files have one line per group, with the following format:

   group_name:::user1,user2,user3 ...


